1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,344
We're pleased to welcome Dr Martin Merry whether of the Antarctic Centre in Christchurch,

2
00:00:05,344 --> 00:00:13,360
New Zealand who has come along to talk to us today about the role of the Centre and the Antarctic Treaty.

3
00:00:13,360 --> 00:00:17,966
Now my first question is about the choice of location for the centre.

4
00:00:17,966 --> 00:00:20,800
Why Christchurch? Was it because of the climate?

5
00:00:20,800 --> 00:00:25,489
Well actually New Zealand is the second closest country to Antarctica

6
00:00:25,489 --> 00:00:28,900
and Christchurch is often used on Antarctic expeditions.

7
00:00:28,900 --> 00:00:32,010
Right, so it's because of where we are...

8
00:00:32,010 --> 00:00:35,600
coupled with our historical role. So tell us

9
00:00:35,940 --> 00:00:37,840
What is the main purpose of the centre?

10
00:00:37,840 --> 00:00:40,220
Well... we have two complementary roles.

11
00:00:40,220 --> 00:00:43,960
One is as a scientific base for expeditions

12
00:00:43,960 --> 00:00:47,360
and research and the other is as an information centre.

13
00:00:47,360 --> 00:00:50,950
Tell us something about the role as a scientific base.

14
00:00:50,950 --> 00:00:57,455
We're able to provide information about what scientists should take with them to the South Pole, for example,

15
00:00:57,455 --> 00:01:01,281
the centre contains a clothing warehouse where expeditions are supplied

16
00:01:01,281 --> 00:01:03,960
with suitable clothing for the extreme conditions.

17
00:01:03,960 --> 00:01:07,180
I suppose you need a bit more than your normal winter coat!

18
00:01:07,180 --> 00:01:12,400
Yes, exactly and then there's also the specialist library and mapping services.

19
00:01:12,400 --> 00:01:17,360
Right. And which countries are actually located at the centre?

20
00:01:17,360 --> 00:01:21,180
Well... the centre houses research programmes for New Zealand

21
00:01:21,700 --> 00:01:23,552
for The United States as well as for Italy...

22
00:01:23,552 --> 00:01:27,680
there's even a US post office at the American airforce base here.

23
00:01:27,680 --> 00:01:31,370
Really? And what does the visitor's centre offer?

24
00:01:31,370 --> 00:01:36,399
Well, since very few people will ever experience the Antarctic first hand,

25
00:01:36,399 --> 00:01:40,590
the visitors' centre aims to recreate the atmosphere of Antarctica.

26
00:01:40,590 --> 00:01:44,640
There's a mock camp site where you can see inside an Antarctic tent

27
00:01:44,640 --> 00:01:46,680
and imagine yourself sleeping there.

28
00:01:46,680 --> 00:01:51,721
And the centre also acts as a showcase for the unique international co-operation

29
00:01:51,721 --> 00:01:53,660
which exists in Antarctica today.

30
00:01:53,660 --> 00:01:56,640
What is it actually like at the South Pole

31
00:01:57,120 --> 00:01:59,850
I know you've been there on a number of occasions.

32
00:01:59,850 --> 00:02:05,673
Yes, I have and each time I'm struck by the awesome beauty of the place.

33
00:02:05,673 --> 00:02:10,720
It's magnificent but you can really only visit it in the summer months.

34
00:02:10,720 --> 00:02:12,350
October to March.

35
00:02:12,350 --> 00:02:16,765
Yes, because it's completely dark for four months of the year (pause)...

36
00:02:16,765 --> 00:02:21,180
and in addition it has to be the coldest place on earth.

37
00:02:21,180 --> 00:02:24,920
Colder than the North Pole? Why's that?

38
00:02:24,920 --> 00:02:29,360
Well, unlike the North Pole, which is actually a frozen sea

39
00:02:29,680 --> 00:02:32,294
Antarctica is a land mass shaped like a dome,

40
00:02:32,294 --> 00:02:39,299
with the result that the winds blow down the slopes at speeds of up to 150 km an hour

41
00:02:39,299 --> 00:02:41,880
and that's what makes it so cold.

42
00:02:41,880 --> 00:02:46,840
And one other interesting thing is that Antarctica is the driest continent on earth,

43
00:02:46,840 --> 00:02:51,800
surprisingly, and so you have to drink large amounts of water when you're there.

44
00:02:51,800 --> 00:02:54,000
How old is Antarctica?

45
00:02:54,000 --> 00:02:57,111
We're pretty sure it was part of a larger land mass

46
00:02:57,111 --> 00:03:01,070
but it broke away from the rest of the continent 170 million years ago.

47
00:03:01,070 --> 00:03:03,000
How can you be certain of this?

48
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:05,997
... because fossils and rocks have been discovered in Antarctica

49
00:03:05,997 --> 00:03:10,660
which are the same as those found in places such as Africa and Australia.

50
00:03:10,660 --> 00:03:15,040
Amazing... To think that it was once attached to Africa...

51
00:03:15,040 --> 00:03:19,140
Now let's just have a look at the Antarctic Treaty

52
00:03:19,880 --> 00:03:23,560
How far back does the idea of an international treaty go?

53
00:03:23,560 --> 00:03:26,920
Well, as far back as the 19th century

54
00:03:27,060 --> 00:03:30,230
when eleven nations organised an international event.

55
00:03:30,230 --> 00:03:32,000
When was that exactly?

56
00:03:32,000 --> 00:03:36,376
In 1870. And it was called the Polar Research Meeting.

57
00:03:36,376 --> 00:03:39,400
And then, not long after that

58
00:03:39,420 --> 00:03:42,940
they organised something called the First International Polar Year.

59
00:03:42,940 --> 00:03:45,760
And that took place when exactly?

60
00:03:45,760 --> 00:03:49,316
Over two years from 1882 to 1883.

61
00:03:49,316 --> 00:03:56,936
But it wasn't until the 1950s that the idea of an international treaty was proposed.

62
00:03:56,936 --> 00:04:01,000
And in 1959 the Treaty was actually signed.

63
00:04:01,000 --> 00:04:04,870
What do you see as the main achievements of the treaty?

64
00:04:04,870 --> 00:04:10,050
Well, firstly it means that the continent is reserved for peaceful use.

65
00:04:10,050 --> 00:04:12,950
That's Article 1, isn't it?

66
00:04:12,950 --> 00:04:13,830
Yes...

67
00:04:13,830 --> 00:04:17,290
That's important since the territory belongs to everyone.

68
00:04:17,290 --> 00:04:20,380
Yes but not as important as Article 5

69
00:04:20,860 --> 00:04:24,180
which prohibits any nuclear explosions or waste disposal.

70
00:04:24,180 --> 00:04:28,015
Which is marvellous. Well, I'm afraid we're going to have to stop there

71
00:04:28,015 --> 00:04:30,375
because I'm afraid we've run out of time.

72
00:04:30,375 --> 00:04:34,800
Thanks for coming along today and telling us all about the centre and its work.
